                <h2>When to Perform Compliance Inspections</h2>
<p>This SOP applies to operations teams running overseas advertising on major platforms such as Meta, Google, and TikTok. It is recommended to conduct inspections at the following intervals:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Daily inspection</strong>: Account balance, campaign status, compliance alerts</li>
<li><strong>Weekly inspection</strong>: Creative compliance, landing page compliance, data anomalies</li>
<li><strong>Monthly inspection</strong>: Account permission settings, policy updates, billing reconciliation</li>
</ul>
<h2>Step 1: Account Status Inspection</h2>
<h3>1.1 Login and Permission Verification</h3>
<p>Before performing any inspection operations, first confirm the following:</p>
<ul>
<li>Ensure the logged-in account has admin or advertiser permissions</li>
<li>Check for any unusual login alerts or device verification prompts</li>
<li>Verify the account is not in "limited advertising" or "under review" status</li>
</ul>
<h3>1.2 Balance and Spend Monitoring</h3>
<ul>
<li>Log into the ad dashboard and verify the account balance matches recharge records</li>
<li>Review the daily spend curve to identify any abnormal spikes</li>
<li>If abnormal balance reduction is detected, immediately investigate for invalid spend or unauthorized use</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Operations Tip</strong>: It is recommended to enable balance alerts that automatically send notifications when the account balance falls below a preset threshold, preventing campaign interruptions due to exhausted funds.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Step 2: Ad Creative Compliance Review</h2>
<h3>2.1 Creative Copy Audit Checklist</h3>
<p>During pre-launch review and routine inspections, verify the following items:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Exaggerated claims</strong>: Avoid absolute language such as "100% effective" or "guaranteed cure"</li>
<li><strong>Price misleading</strong>: Clearly indicate price units (e.g., USD) and avoid misleading discount statements</li>
<li><strong>Privacy data</strong>: No user avatars, names, contact information, or other unauthorized data in creatives</li>
<li><strong>Sensitive elements</strong>: Check for involvement of political figures, religious symbols, violence, or graphic content</li>
</ul>
<h3>2.2 Landing Page Compliance Verification</h3>
<ul>
<li>Confirm the landing page includes a valid privacy policy link</li>
<li>Review form collection fields to ensure they do not exceed functional necessity</li>
<li>Verify page load speed and ensure mobile accessibility</li>
</ul>
<h2>Step 3: Campaign and Targeting Compliance</h2>
<h3>3.1 Audience Targeting Legality Check</h3>
<ul>
<li>Confirm targeting does not involve protected attributes (such as race, religion, sexual orientation)</li>
<li>Verify the source of remarketing audience lists for compliance</li>
<li>Avoid expanding "Lookalike Audiences" to sensitive groups</li>
</ul>
<h3>3.2 Campaign Category Compliance</h3>
<p>Based on the product type being advertised, verify category-specific requirements:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Financial products</strong>: Confirm possession of relevant licenses or disclosures for target markets</li>
<li><strong>Healthcare products</strong>: Prohibit efficacy claims; add necessary disclaimers</li>
<li><strong>Gaming/gambling products</strong>: Verify target markets allow such content advertising</li>
</ul>
<h2>Step 4: Policy Update Confirmation</h2>
<p>Platform policies are updated periodically. It is recommended to follow updates through:</p>
<ol>
<li>Subscribe to official platform policy update notification emails</li>
<li>Regularly review platform community guidelines or help centers</li>
<li>Follow industry media policy interpretation articles</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Operations Tip</strong>: A utility app team once failed to adjust creatives after a policy update, resulting in high-spending ads being blocked for compliance violations and unnecessary budget waste. It is recommended to include policy updates in weekly review meetings.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Step 5: Financial Reconciliation and Anomaly Investigation</h2>
<h3>5.1 Spend Data Cross-Referencing</h3>
<ul>
<li>Export ad platform spend reports</li>
<li>Cross-reference with third-party attribution platform data</li>
<li>Check for situations with long periods of no spend but continued billing</li>
</ul>
<h3>5.2 Abnormal Spend Handling Process</h3>
<p>If the following anomalies are detected, handle in order:</p>
<ol>
<li>Screenshot and save abnormal data</li>
<li>Contact platform support or代理商 (agency) to submit an appeal</li>
<li>Pause relevant ad sets to minimize losses</li>
<li>Document the cause of the anomaly in the inspection log</li>
</ol>
<h2>Inspection Log Template</h2>
<p>It is recommended that operations teams use the following format to record each inspection:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Inspection date</strong>: YYYY-MM-DD</li>
<li><strong>Inspector</strong>: Name/Employee ID</li>
<li><strong>Accounts inspected</strong>: Number of accounts covered</li>
<li><strong>Issues found</strong>: Specific problems and severity levels</li>
<li><strong>Status</strong>: Resolved / In Progress / Pending Follow-up</li>
<li><strong>Notes</strong>: Additional explanations or improvement suggestions</li>
</ul>
<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p>Compliance inspection is foundational to overseas advertising success, not a one-time task. It is recommended that all operations teams incorporate these inspection items into daily workflows, establishing systematic and periodic inspection practices to ensure account security while improving overall campaign efficiency.</p>
